import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="My plan, and what's not in it — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="Where LightPhotos is going. Your photos stay on your drive, AI runs on your machine, and only the table-stakes features get ported to every platform."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-09-20">September 20, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>My plan, and what's not in it</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          Where LightPhotos is going. Your photos stay on your drive, AI runs
          on your machine, and only the table-stakes features get ported to
          every platform.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 6 min read</p>
      </header>

      <p>
        I've written a fair amount about how LightPhotos works. I have not
        written down where it's going, and I keep making that call one feature
        at a time, which is how a small tool quietly turns into a large one.
      </p>
      <p>
        So here is the plan. The useful half is what's not in it. This is the
        same trick as the seven-item list in
        <a href="/blogs/why-i-started-lightphotos.html">the first post</a>,
        except now the list is about the next year rather than the first
        afternoon.
      </p>

      <h2>Your photos stay yours</h2>
      <p>
        I'm not building a platform to store your photos. No account, no
        upload, no library I hold on your behalf. Your photos are files on your
        drive, and LightPhotos opens the folder they're already in.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is not a philosophical position so much as one I arrived at the
        hard way, after losing years of photos to a library format only one app
        could read. I wrote about that in the first post and it still decides
        most of these calls.
      </p>
      <p>
        I do want to reach drives that aren't physically attached, Google Drive
        first. The blocker there is speed, not principle. A photo tool that
        stalls every time you arrow to the next frame isn't worth shipping, so
        this happens only if the performance turns out to be acceptable. I
        don't know yet whether it will be.
      </p>

      <h2>I'm not cloning Lightroom</h2>
      <p>
        Lightroom has a lot of genuinely good features and I'm not going to
        match them. The AI ones in particular are the strongest part of the
        product, and nearly all of them work by sending your photo to somebody
        else's cloud platform. That is the one thing I've said I won't do.
      </p>
      <p>
        What I want to look at instead is ComfyUI, running locally on your own
        machine. I haven't built any of this yet, so treat it as something I
        want to investigate rather than something I've promised. The appeal is
        that the model runs where the photos already are, and nothing leaves
        your drive.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="lp-pullquote">
        The interesting question isn't whether AI belongs in a photo editor. It
        is whether it can run without your photos leaving the machine.
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Not every feature on every platform</h2>
      <p>
        Holding every feature to the lowest common denominator across four
        platforms means shipping less, and shipping the blandest version of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I hold the two halves to different standards. The table-stakes
        features already run everywhere, in the browser and on Windows and
        Linux as well as the Mac. Browsing a folder, rating, filtering, the
        basic adjustments and export are all in that group, and anything of
        that kind I build next joins them.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 216" role="img" aria-label="Two panels. The left panel, table stakes, lists macOS, Windows, Linux and browser, with browsing, rating, filtering, adjustments and export from one Rust codebase compiled to WASM. The right panel, Mac only, lists Core Image, Vision and VisionKit, Image I/O, AVFoundation and Object Capture as Apple frameworks that are hard to port.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WHAT RUNS WHERE</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="24" width="380" height="180" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="48">Table stakes</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="66">One Rust codebase, compiled to WASM for the browser</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="80" width="78" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="55" y="97" text-anchor="middle">macOS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="106" y="80" width="78" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
