        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The left panel is already built and runs on all four. The right panel
          is an experiment, and anything that lands there may stay Mac-only for
          a long time.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Above that line, I want to find out what Apple's native frameworks are
        worth. Core Image, Vision and VisionKit, Image I/O, AVFoundation, and
        Object Capture for photogrammetry. I already know from
        <a href="/blogs/what-imageio-gives-you-free.html">porting away from
        Image I/O</a> exactly how this goes. You get a great deal of work for
        free, and then you cannot take any of it with you.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'm going in with that priced in this time. Anything built on those
        frameworks will likely be Mac-only, possibly permanently, and I would
        rather have a feature on one platform than not have it at all.
      </p>

      <h2>Getting your photos out</h2>
      <p>
        Refusing to be your library only works if it's easy to hand your photos
        to something that is. So I plan to support Immich, and exporting to a
        cloud target more generally.
      </p>
      <p>
        The part I care about is that export works the same way against a
        server running on your own network and against a real remote one. Those
        should not be two features with one of them half-finished.
      </p>

      <h2>Everything from the keyboard</h2>
      <p>
        Every feature should be reachable from the keyboard, and you should be
        able to run the entire app without touching the mouse. Not the common
        ones, not most of them. All of them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Part of this is that keyboard shortcuts are what I wanted from a photo
        tool in the first place, and they're why Luminar didn't fit how I work.
        The other part is that when I
        <a href="/blogs/why-rust.html">chose to build everything in Rust</a> I
        gave up browser-grade accessibility, and I said so at the time.
        Keyboard coverage is the piece of that gap I can close myself, so I'm
        treating it as a requirement rather than a nice-to-have.
      </p>

      <h2>Open source, all of it</h2>
      <p>
        I plan to open source the entire codebase. Not a core library with the
        interesting parts held back, and not a delayed drop of an old version.
        The whole app.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the same argument as the storage one, one level down. I'm
        asking you to trust that your photos stay on your drive and that
        nothing gets uploaded. You should be able to check that yourself
        instead of taking my word for it.
      </p>

      <h2>The next app works the same way</h2>
      <p>
        This plan is bigger than one photo editor. I want to build other Rust
        applications the same way, and by now the shape is deliberate rather
        than accidental.
      </p>
      <p>
        Build a native Mac app first, because a Mac is what I use and I would
        rather aim the first version at a user I understand completely. Then
        take the basic functionality to WASM so it runs in a browser, and from
        there to the other platforms people actually have.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightPhotos is the app where I worked that sequence out. Forking low
        and converging high, keeping the core in its own module away from the
        UI, pricing a platform framework before depending on it. Those were all
        lessons from this codebase, and they're the parts I expect to carry
        over unchanged.
      </p>

      <h2>The plan on one page</h2>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col">Not doing</th>
